U.S.
Source: U.S. Bureau of the Census, Census of the Population

1. In 1950, the definition of "urban" was changed. Data shown here for urban and rural population in 1950 are comparable with later data. Typically, the change in definition raised the urban population numbers by 5-15%.

2. Alaska census for 1940 was taken on October 1, 1939 and census for 1930 on October 1, 1929.

3. Total population for New Mexico in 1860 includes population of area taken to form part of Arizona Territory in 1863.

4. Population for Territory of New Mexico in 1850 includes parts of present states of Arizona, and New Mexico, and smaller parts of Colorado and Nevada.

5. Total population for New York in 1830 includes 5,602 persons for whom sex information is not available.

6. In 1860-1880, North and South Dakota comprised the Dakota Territory. Sex detail for South Dakota included with North Dakota.

7. Total population figures for Oregon in 1850 have been reduced by the population total of those parts of the Oregon Territory taken to form part of the Washington Territory in 1853 and 1859. Sex detail for Washington included with Oregon.

8. Sex detail for West Virginia, 1820-1860 included with Virginia.

9. Total population for Washington in 1860 includes population of Idaho and parts of Montana and Wyoming.
␀Notes on Population Data

Canada
Source: Statistics Canada, "Historical Statistics of 
Canada," 2nd Edition.

1. Each census figure has been rounded independently to the nearest hundred.  

2. Urban population in 1951 includes a few metropolitan area parts with fewer than 1,000 population.  

3. Populations for Northwest Territories and Yukon in 1991 are estimated values.  

4. No population data available for Newfoundland prior to 1949 when it became a province.
